Hi Hans,

Now I have briefly tested booting from the Tru64 V5.1A O/S CD.
My system which I used for testing is an AS1200 and not an AS800, but 
they are about similar age. I have an AS800 as well, but I didn't have 
time to dig that out from my warehouse for the moment.
Anyway, the problem you were encountering was regarding the KZPAC and 
not the system itself.

My findings are that the Tru64 installation kernel finds the KZPAC and 
the LUNs behind it (DRA0 and DRA1) while booting:

.
.
.
Loading SIOP: script c0000000, reg 7fe6e00, data c000a000
scsi0 at psiop0 slot 0 rad 0
Initializing xcr0.  Please wait....
xcr0 at pci1 slot 3
RAID-ctlr-0-unit-0 at xcr0 unit 0 (unit status = ONLINE, raid level = 1)
         (WRITE BACK cache operation SUPPORTED if battery backup enabled)
RAID-ctlr-0-unit-1 at xcr0 unit 1 (unit status = ONLINE, raid level = 5)
         (WRITE BACK cache operation SUPPORTED if battery backup enabled)
isp0 at pci1 slot 4
isp0: QLOGIC ISP1040B/V2
isp0: Firmware revision 5.57 (loaded by console)
isp0: Fast RAM timing enabled.
.
.
.

What I had forgot was that it doesn't show the old type of device names 
(re0 or re1) anymore.

When the boot had reached the single-user mode it found the disks 
through hwmgr. But not through scu (that's what I've forgot as well):

# hwmgr -view devi
  HWID: Device Name          Mfg      Model            Location
 
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
     3: (unknown)
     4: (unknown)                     (unknown)        (unknown)
     5: (unknown)
    18: /dev/cport/scp0               SWXCR            xcr0
    19: /dev/disk/dsk0c               SWXCR            ctlr-0-unit-0
    20: /dev/disk/dsk1c               SWXCR            ctlr-0-unit-1
    43: /dev/disk/floppy0c            3.5in floppy     fdi0-unit-0
    46: /dev/disk/cdrom0c    DEC      RRD46   (C) DEC  bus-0-targ-5-lun-0


Then I continued with the installation script up to the point where the 
installation disk is to be selected:

Select a disk for the root file system.  The root file system will be
placed on the "a" partition of the disk you choose.

To visually locate a disk, enter "ping <disk>", where <disk> is the device
name (for example, dsk0) of the disk you want to locate.  If that disk has
a visible indicator light, it will blink until you are ready to continue.
       Device      Size  Controller  Disk
       Name       in GB  Type        Model           Location
1)    dsk0         4.0  RAID        SWXCR           ctlr-0-unit-0
2)    dsk1        12.0  RAID        SWXCR           ctlr-0-unit-1

After this I didn't continue further, because I have another 
installation (VMS) on the disks and don't want to spoil i t right now.

Summa summarum, I think the Tru64 V5.1A installation is fully possible 
on KZPAC mirror or RAID sets. Why you didn't succeed to get the LUNs to 
show up during the installation is something I cannot answer right now, 
but maybe there is some kind of configuration matter which would need to 
be cleared out to get the LUNs visible.
If you like, I can try to help you further with the troubleshooting.


Kari




